From: theycallmeboxy <82409831+theycallmeboxy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:50:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] panvk: decode mutable BC4/BC5/BC6H images for both
 signednesses

vkd3d-proton creates every DXGI BC4/BC5/BC6H_TYPELESS texture as a
MUTABLE_FORMAT image of the class's first member (BC4_UNORM, BC5_UNORM,
BC6H_UFLOAT) with a format list of both members, and the shader resource
views pick UNORM or SNORM (UF16 or SF16). With BC emulation, uploads decode
the blocks once, as the image's own format, and every BC view samples that
copy. A view with the other signedness therefore read the unsigned decode:
a BC4_SNORM view of a BC4_UNORM image returned 1.0 where the blocks decode
to -0.70, and BC6H SF16 views returned the UF16 values. Signed and unsigned
blocks don't decode into each other (the endpoint order selects a
different mode, endpoints are sign-extended), so the existing copy can't
serve both.

Give such images a second decoded copy, decoded as the other member: a
MUTABLE image of these formats whose format list names the other member, or
that has no list, gets bc_alt. Its copy is laid out after the first one
(as bc_alt_shadow on the fragment-decode path, a LINEAR plane on the compute
path), counted in the memory requirements, bound with the image, written by
every decode (uploads, copies, zero-initialised transitions), and views in
the bc_alt format sample it. Such images keep their raw blocks with
PANVK_BC_DROP_RAW, since the second copy is decoded from them. UNORM/SRGB
classes (BC1/2/3/7) need nothing: their decoded storage already serves both
views. Images that aren't mutable, or list only one signedness, are
unchanged.

drawcpu bcmut (512x512 BC textures, 10 levels, created as vkd3d does,
levels 0 and 1 sampled through each view and compared with a plain image of
the view format): BC4/BC5 SNORM views of UNORM images and the reverse go
from 480-512 of 512 texels wrong to exact, BC6H SF16 views of UF16 images
and the reverse likewise, on the fragment and the compute decode path
(PANVK_DEBUG=bc_compute); BC1/2/3/7 UNORM and SRGB views and non-mutable
images unchanged. The second copy costs memory: such a 512x512 image takes
930 KB instead of 553 KB (BC4), 1.80 MB instead of 1.08 MB (BC5) and
4.81 MB instead of 1.78 MB (BC6H UF16, whose SF16 copy is RGBA16F).

